    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты с чего взял?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where did you get that?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'you' with 'got'. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Ещё один нелегал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Another illegal.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'more' directly with 'illegal immigrant'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Господи, куда вас девать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Lord, where to put you?' Chef Viktor uses the polite plural 'you' alongside 'to put', keeping a formal or respectful register.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы отсидитесь в туалете.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You will sit in the toilet.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'you' with 'the toilet'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Потом уйдёте через вход.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then leave through the entrance.' Chef Viktor combines 'then' with 'entrance' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Они дежурят на входе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They are on guard at the entrance.' Max links 'they' directly with 'to be on duty' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сядет в зал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He will sit in the hall.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'to sit down' with 'the hall'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сходи в бар, возьми одежду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go to the bar, take clothes.' Chef Viktor links 'go' directly with 'bar' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Терпи, давай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Endure, come on.' Here 'bear' sets up the idea, while 'let's' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Поздравляю вас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I congratulate you.' the inspector combines 'congratulations' with 'you' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "У вас все чисто.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everything is clean with you.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'at' with 'clean'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я же вам сразу сказал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I told you right away.' Chef Viktor uses the polite plural 'you' here alongside 'I', keeping a formal or respectful tone.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Я не имею права верить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have no right to believe.' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'to believe'. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
